  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100959,
      "id": "80ed79a9-8e39-5c84-9c87-dec266060afc",
      "title": "Identifying Solutions to First-Order Differential Equations"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "04c3ff5b-105a-5861-944a-99ecf1c464ee",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2 y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y=2 x^{2}$\n- **II.** $y=- 4 x^{2}$\n- **III.** $y=- 3 x^{3} - 2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the stated domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2 x^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4 x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ 4 x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(2 x^{2}\\right)}{x} \\\\ 4 x & =4 x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2 x^{2}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 4 x^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 8 x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - 8 x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(- 4 x^{2}\\right)}{x} \\\\ - 8 x & =- 8 x.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=- 4 x^{2}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=- 3 x^{3} - 2,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=- 9 x^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2 y}{x} \\\\ - 9 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot \\left(- 3 x^{3} - 2\\right)}{x} \\\\ - 9 x^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2 \\left(- 3 x^{3} - 2\\right)}{x} \\\\ \\frac{4 - 3 x^{3}}{x} & \\not\\equiv 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=- 3 x^{3} - 2$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "94e0fde1-777d-550e-9091-d8ad16b2ea0e",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
